// src/v4/std/verification.dag
//
// Scope: TestClaim schema — tests are data, not code. Modeled fresh
//        against the v4 substrate (v3 dsl/std/verification.dag studied
//        for design, not lifted — its TestClaim carried no input/expected
//        and a different AssertKind set).
// Anchor: THESIS.md "Tests are structural data" §348-368 + Self-hosting facet 3
//
// Owns:
//   - TestClaim { kind: AssertKind, label: String, input: Node,
//                 expected: Outcome<Node> }
//   - AssertKind = Equals | Diagnostic | Compiles | RoundTrips   (closed —
//     the complete assertion set. Tier1 = Compiles | Diagnostic; Tier2 =
//     Equals; the self-host / format fixed point = RoundTrips. NO "..." —
//     a 5th kind at fill-time is a substrate-extension STOP, not a silent
//     open enum.)
//   - Boundary/simulation does NOT add an AssertKind. A simulated external
//     dependency is generated Node bound into `input` (program ∘ generated
//     simulator) — see lens/testgen.dag simulator-generation arm. The
//     assertion shape stays one of the closed 4; only `input` changes.
//   - no hand-Rust tests; every test predicate IS a TestClaim instance
//
// Consumes:
//   - std/node.dag: Node (`input`, and the `Produced` value carried by
//     `expected`). Conj/Disj are the record/sum surface sugar.
//   - std/diagnostic.dag: Outcome<T> (the `expected` carrier). The
//     scaffold's "Diagnostic for failure mode" is reached THROUGH
//     `Outcome.Rejected { diagnostic: Diagnostic }` — see HEADER
//     RECONCILE below.
//   (String for label is the kernel-ambient String — no import.)
//
// Status: T-3 modeled 2026-05-16 (cool-wren-237).
// Brief: see BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md; this file's I/O contract is the immutable
// contract for the worker. Splitting this file requires explicit operator
// ratification (substrate extension = stop signal).
//
// HEADER RECONCILE (operator decision I, 2026-05-16). The original
// scaffold left `input` and `expected` DELIBERATELY UNTYPED while `kind`
// and `label` were typed — the tell that their faithful type was a then-
// pending substrate decision. That decision is the value-or-Diagnostic
// carrier STOP (surfaced from T-4.6; this session independently
// confirmed TestClaim.expected as a canonical consumer). The operator
// ratified decision I: `Outcome<T> = Produced { value: T } | Rejected
// { diagnostic: Diagnostic }`, landed in std/diagnostic.dag (#3181,
// merged). The previously-untyped fields are now realized:
//   - `input: Node` — per the scaffold's OWN "generated Node bound into
//     `input`" framing (boundary-sim = program ∘ generated simulator).
//     (Interpretation note: the dispatch read "type expected (and input)
//     via Outcome<T>"; `input` is typed `Node`, not `Outcome<Node>` —
//     the input is the program under test, not a produced outcome; the
//     scaffold's "generated Node into `input`" is decisive. Surfaced for
//     conscious confirmation, not silently chosen.)
//   - `expected: Outcome<Node>` — the asserted result of running `input`;
//     `kind` selects how it is compared (see AssertKind ledger).
// `Outcome<T>` is CONSUMED from std/diagnostic.dag, never re-invented
// here (the carrier-STOP's binding rule: one ratified spine, no per-file
// shape). Consuming `Outcome` rather than bare `Diagnostic` fulfils the
// scaffold's "Diagnostic for failure mode" — the failure mode is
// `Outcome.Rejected`'s `Diagnostic`. `Node` + `Outcome` in Consumes are
// the mechanical consequence of the ratified typing of the deferred
// fields, not a scope expansion; no other scaffold-header content
// changed. This realization is operator-ratified + dispatch-directed,
// surfaced in review — not a unilateral worker edit (diagnostic.dag
// #3161 / witness.dag #3178 reconcile precedent).


module v4.std.verification


import v4.std.node { Node }
import v4.std.diagnostic { Outcome }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Modeling notes (read before validating against the anchor)
//
// SCHEMA FILE. verification.dag declares the test-as-data shape — it
// owns no producer logic. lens/testgen.dag (T-19) PRODUCES `TestClaim`
// values from the substrate and compiler/05_eval.dag (T-22) EVALUATES
// them; this file is the shape they construct to. There is deliberately
// no `run_claim` / `assert` helper here — a consumer reads a `TestClaim`
// by `match`ing `kind` and comparing the produced `Outcome<Node>`
// against `expected` (THESIS §348-368 "tests are structural data").
//
// WHY `expected: Outcome<Node>` UNIFIES THE CLOSED 4. Running `input`
// (a Node program, possibly program ∘ generated simulator) yields an
// `Outcome<Node>` — `Produced { value }` or `Rejected { diagnostic }`.
// The four assertion FORMS are not four payload types; they are four
// comparison semantics over that ONE result type, selected by `kind`:
//   - Equals      — assert `Produced { value }` and `value` structurally
//                   equals `expected`'s produced value (Tier-2).
//   - Diagnostic  — assert `Rejected { diagnostic }` and the diagnostic
//                   matches `expected`'s rejected diagnostic (Tier-1).
//   - Compiles    — assert the result is `Produced` at all; the produced
//                   value is not compared (a unary success assertion —
//                   `expected` carries the canonical `Produced` form,
//                   `kind` says "only the Produced/Rejected discriminant
//                   is load-bearing"). Tier-1.
//   - RoundTrips  — assert `Produced { value }` and `value` structurally
//                   equals `input` (emit∘ingest fixed point / self-host
//                   bit-identity). `expected` carries that produced
//                   `≈input` value.
// KNOWN STRUCTURAL TENSION (api-review #3183, openai-pro — surfaced,
// not claimed away). `kind` and `expected` are INDEPENDENT fields, so
// the frozen scaffold shape `TestClaim { kind, label, input, expected }`
// + payload-less closed-4 `AssertKind` DOES admit the kind×expected
// cross-product: e.g. `kind: Diagnostic` with `expected: Produced{…}`,
// or `kind: Compiles` with an arbitrary/`Rejected` expectation, is
// representable. The kind↔expected-variant correspondence (Equals/
// RoundTrips ⟹ `Produced`; Diagnostic ⟹ `Rejected`; Compiles ⟹
// `Produced`-discriminant, value vestigial) is therefore an EVALUATOR-
// ENFORCED semantic contract (compiler/05_eval.dag, T-22), NOT
// structurally enforced at this schema boundary. This file does not
// claim otherwise. The structurally-enforcing form folds the
// expectation into a single assertion sum so malformed states are
// unconstructable — but that (a) changes the FROZEN scaffold's
// `TestClaim`/`AssertKind` shape (operator-tier; not a unilateral
// worker edit — the header is the immutable contract), and (b) is
// entangled with the carrier-STOP (a `Produced`/`Rejected`-shaped
// per-variant expectation risks re-instantiating the value-or-
// Diagnostic spine this file was explicitly required to consume, not
// re-derive — prior api-review APPROVE blessed exactly the consume-
// Outcome shape). The two api-reviews conflict on this exact point.
// Resolution is escalated to the T-3 manager / operator; this note is
// the honest tracked statement of the gap, not a silent convention.
//
// The one coproduct below (`AssertKind`) carries the full Practice-4
// 🟢/🟡/🔴 classification + the five-pattern dissolution ledger
// (modeling-discipline.md §4 — operator-ratified binding 2026-05-15;
// the doc enumerates FIVE patterns, the fifth being Parameterized-
// family). `TestClaim` is a Conj record — no ledger applies.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// AssertKind — the closed set of structural-test assertion FORMS: HOW a
// TestClaim asserts its produced `Outcome<Node>` relates to `expected`.
// Four irreducible forms at the testing-discipline boundary (THESIS
// §348-368): value-equality, expected-diagnostic, compilation-success,
// round-trip fixed point. CLOSED — a 5th form at fill-time is a
// substrate-extension STOP (scaffold header), routed to operator
// ratification, never a silent open enum.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — classification: 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger —
// five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: the single consumer (the TestClaim
//      evaluator, compiler/05_eval.dag) dispatches on ALL four to pick
//      the comparison; it is the shared assertion-discriminant, not a
//      per-consumer scattered fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: the four are nullary form-tags whose
//      payload already lives in `expected: Outcome<Node>`. Promoting to
//      `{ form: Symbol }` replaces a closed exhaustive 4-set with an
//      open opaque identity (`form` = an unmodelled spelling becomes
//      representable) — violates P2 + the closed-set rule (a closed
//      assertion set is an enum, not a Symbol proxy). The 4-variant
//      enum IS the faithful form; the comparison semantics per form are
//      genuinely distinct (value-eq vs diagnostic-match vs produced-
//      discriminant vs input-fixedpoint), not one shape relabelled.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: an assertion form is not an algebra
//      carrier; the four do not trace to a richer std/algebra.dag
//      structure (no Monoid/Order they inhabit). They ARE the
//      irreducible partition of "ways a structural test asserts",
//      grounded at the THESIS testing boundary — no richer std/ source.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a TestClaim asserts EXACTLY ONE form —
//      value-equality OR expected-diagnostic OR compiles OR round-trips;
//      mutually-exclusive author-chosen contracts, never simultaneous
//      coordinates in a product of independent axes. No claim is "both
//      Equals and RoundTrips".
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the four are not `F<X>` over a
//      separately-declared enumerable set (Practice 7). They are the
//      fixed assertion-form partition; there is no source set they are
//      a mechanical copy of, and no generic / projection collapses them.
//      (`Outcome<Node>`'s genericity is the RESULT carrier's, not an
//      enumerated-copy of these forms.)
// Terminal: the irreducible closed set of structural-test assertion
// forms at the testing-discipline boundary; a 5th is a substrate-
// extension STOP (scaffold header), not a worker addition.
type AssertKind
  = Equals
  | Diagnostic
  | Compiles
  | RoundTrips


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// TestClaim — one test, as data (THESIS §348-368). Not a record of a
// test run; the test ITSELF. A consumer (compiler/05_eval.dag, T-22)
// runs `input`, obtains an `Outcome<Node>`, and compares it to
// `expected` under the semantics `kind` selects.
//
//   kind      — the assertion FORM (which comparison; see AssertKind).
//   label     — human-readable description; surfaces as the failure
//               message when the claim does not hold. Kernel-ambient
//               `String` (scaffold header — not imported).
//   input     — the Node program under test (boundary-sim composes a
//               generated simulator Node into it: program ∘ simulator;
//               the assertion shape is unchanged, only `input` differs).
//   expected  — the asserted result of running `input`, as the operator-
//               ratified `Outcome<Node>` carrier (Produced value vs
//               Rejected diagnostic). Consumed from std/diagnostic.dag,
//               never re-invented (carrier-STOP binding rule).
//
// This is a Conj record, not a coproduct — no Practice-4 ledger applies
// (records do not dissolve to a richer source; the diagnostic.dag
// `Diagnostic` precedent).
type TestClaim {
  kind: AssertKind
  label: String
  input: Node
  expected: Outcome<Node>
}
